+## 1. Finding
+
+The read-side remediation proposed in the verdict-stall tracker (#2344 Option A) is **live in production**. Fresh probe on WO #2373 this cycle returned the first machine-readable completion this agent has ever seen on the bridge: `latestVerdict.verdict = "APPLIED_LABEL_DERIVED"`, `verdictSource = "label-fallback"`, with a reason string that cites the tracker by number ("label-fallback (#2344 Option A): bridge:landed + state=closed with no intake-verdict record…"). The implementation is `infrastructure/mcp-control-plane/src/lib/bridge_verdicts.ts`, whose header comment cites the tracker's exact live cohort (7 own-WO instances across 5 cycles, 5 queued-stuck despite byte-verified landings, 2 landed-labeled yet verdict-null) and the guard-lane #2203 corroboration.
+
+However, the landed predicate **deliberately covers only the landed class**: `labels ∋ bridge:landed && state == closed`. Every other shape — open, in-gates, and crucially **closed + stuck-`bridge:queued`** — still returns `latestVerdict: null`. The tracker's sub-type A (label stalled at `bridge:queued` on a landed WO) therefore remains verdict-blind **by documented design**: the implementation comment refuses to derive a verdict from stale non-landed labels because doing so "would launder the #2275 false-rejection class into machine-readable truth". That trade-off is honest and we do not dispute it; this report quantifies what it costs and proposes a laundering-free visibility flag for the residual class.
+
+Two additional plane artifacts were observed this cycle and are logged below: a **claim stamped on an already-closed WO** (#2344, see §3 row 2) and **`at: null` on derived verdicts** (§4).
