+## 4. Residual gap + two new artifacts
+
+**Residual class cost.** With Option A live, the landed-labeled class (tracker sub-type B) is now machine-readable and its raw-fetch verification cost is eliminated. The remaining cost is bounded to the queued-stall class (sub-type A): for rows whose label never advances past `bridge:queued`, agents must still raw-fetch + byte-compare to know a landing happened (#2344 is the live exemplar at probe time). The tracker's N-agent × M-WO cost estimate therefore shrinks but does not reach zero; the tracker's Option B (intake verdict writer at the tower, HITL deploy lane) remains the only root fix that covers all classes.
+
+**Artifact 1 — claim stamped on a closed WO.** #2344's `latestClaim` (claude-orion-c23, 00:34:41Z) was written after the work was already byte-verified on main (≤00:30Z), on a WO that is now closed, with a 30-min TTL long expired. This is the inverse direction of the stale-claim class the freshly landed loop guard documents (board labels lag live claims; here a live claim rides a stale/closed row). The new loop's STALE-CLAIM GUARD already mandates feedback-plane re-verification immediately before `work_claim` and no-retry-on-atomic-rejection; #2344 is a live demonstration of why that guard is necessary, and tower-side it suggests claim stamps are accepted for rows in any state — worth a cheap guard on the claim write path (reject claims on `state=closed` rows).
+
+**Artifact 2 — derived verdicts carry `at: null`.** The fallback derives `latestVerdict` from state + labels but leaves the timestamp unset. Minor: consumers cannot distinguish "derived 5 minutes ago" from "derived 5 days ago" without an external clock, and the derivation can flip if a label is corrected later (history stays empty by design). A derivation timestamp (read-side `at` = when the fallback computed it, or none if purity is preferred) would make the derived record self-describing. Cosmetic, non-blocking.
+
+**Not defects (checked, honest):** the purity choice (`intakeVerdict: null` preserved, history unpolluted) is correct and matches the tracker's auditability requirement; the refusal to derive from `bridge:rejected` labels is correct (#2275 class protection); compile + dependency + signature checks on the new loop all pass (§3 row 6); `scripts/queue_consistency_probe.py` being mirror-absent while cited in loop comments is already corrected by #2422's architecture note — not re-filed here.
+
+## 5. Remediation options for the residual class
+
+- **Option A3 (read-side, no write-path risk, laundering-free):** extend the read model with a distinct non-verdict field — e.g. `labelStall: true` — set when `state == closed` and no terminal label (`bridge:landed` / `bridge:rejected` / `bridge:idempotent-noop`) is present. It asserts only the fact "label plane stalled on a closed row" (always true when set), never invents APPLIED/REJECTED semantics, so the #2275 laundering concern does not apply. Agents could then filter discovery on the flag instead of probing every closed-queued row, cutting the residual raw-fetch cost to rows already flagged. Precedent-shaped like the tracker's Option A: pure, offline-derivable, testable against the same cohort.
+- **Option B (root fix, unchanged from tracker):** intake verdict writer at the tower so anonymous-bridge WOs get real APPLIED/REJECTED records at intake time; HITL deploy lane (#2225/#2226 precedent). Covers all classes including queued-stall; supersedes A3 if scheduled.
+- **Status quo (documented trade-off):** acceptable short-term — cost is now bounded to the queued-stall class, and the freshly landed loop guard already forces feedback-plane re-verification before claims, which keeps agents honest on exactly these rows.
+
+This report does not request code changes from the agent lane: the fallback lives in the tower control plane (HITL deploy lane), and every option above is owner-side.
